WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.709
The lobbying industrial complex.

00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:04.579
Influence for sale.

00:00:04.579 --> 00:00:08.875
Over five thousand active lobbyists registered with the federal government.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:16.375
Over thirteen thousand communication reports filed annually, each documenting a meeting between a lobbyist and a public office holder.

00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:26.272
The pharmaceutical industry, telecommunications companies, energy corporations, defence contractors, and financial institutions maintain permanent lobbying operations in Ottawa.

00:00:26.272 --> 00:00:28.727
These are not occasional meetings.

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:37.397
They are a continuous pipeline of influence that operates every working day between industry and the government officials who set the policies affecting those industries.

00:00:37.397 --> 00:00:43.181
The lobbying registry documents this — names, dates, subjects discussed, offices contacted.

00:00:43.181 --> 00:00:45.113
The data is public.

00:00:45.113 --> 00:00:52.590
The pattern is consistent: the industries with the highest lobbying activity receive the most favourable regulatory and policy outcomes.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:57.852
The five sectors with the highest lobbying activity and their documented policy outcomes.

00:00:57.852 --> 00:00:59.909
The maintenance mechanism.

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:03.772
Lobbying is the maintenance mechanism of institutional capture.

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:07.534
The captured system does not sustain itself automatically.

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:17.511
It requires continuous investment in influence to maintain regulatory outcomes, shape legislation, and ensure that the revolving door between government and industry remains open.

00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:23.136
The five thousand registered lobbyists represent the visible portion of this influence infrastructure.

00:01:23.136 --> 00:01:31.011
Government relations firms, industry associations, think tanks funded by industry, and political donations form the broader ecosystem.

00:01:31.011 --> 00:01:38.113
The asymmetry is structural: industries invest millions in lobbying because the policy outcomes they secure are worth billions.

00:01:38.113 --> 00:01:40.818
Citizens have zero lobbying budget.

00:01:40.818 --> 00:01:44.511
The lobbying registry documents who meets whom about what.

00:01:44.511 --> 00:01:47.534
The policy outcomes document who wins.

00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:56.147
The correlation between lobbying activity and favourable policy outcomes is the documented evidence that institutional capture is maintained, not accidental.
